  <h2 id="gates">Pass The 3 Gates before you spend real time or money on this</h2>

  <p>
    The 4-Checkpoint Protocol tells you which lane fits on paper. The 3 Gates make you test it in the
    real world before you commit a course fee, a career switch, or a full year to it.
  </p>

  <div class="gates-block">
    <p class="gates-block__title">
      Do not commit to a full course or a mid-career switch before passing all three gates in your chosen
      lane.
    </p>
    <div class="gates-row">
      <div class="gate">
        <span class="gate__num">Gate 1</span>
        <span class="gate__name">Proof of skill</span>
        <p>
          For content, write one real piece for a real, even tiny, audience — a guest post, a LinkedIn
          article, or a piece for a small business you know. For SEO, run one real technical audit on one
          real website and document the actual issues you find, not a tutorial clone everyone else has
          already used.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="gate">
        <span class="gate__num">Gate 2</span>
        <span class="gate__name">Proof of communication</span>
        <p>
          Explain in under two minutes, in plain language, why your piece is worth reading or why your
          audit fix should matter to a business. If you can only explain the mechanics, not the outcome it
          supports, you are not ready to sell this in an interview.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="gate">
        <span class="gate__num">Gate 3</span>
        <span class="gate__name">Proof of value</span>
        <p>
          Show the work to a working content marketer or SEO professional and ask one direct question:
          "Would this get shortlisted at your company?" Use their answer, not your own hope, to decide.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="switching-later">Can you switch between them later?</h2>

  <p>
    Yes, and the switch happens constantly through the SEO content strategy lane described above, which is
    one more reason not to treat this as a permanent, irreversible fork in the road.
  </p>

  <p>
    Writers moving toward SEO-heavy roles usually need a focused stretch of keyword research, basic
    technical SEO, and one audit tool, plus a real project applying it to their own or a client's site,
    before the switch becomes credible to employers. Their existing understanding of what makes content
    worth reading is a real head start; what they are missing is technical and data-tool fluency, not
    editorial judgment.
  </p>

  <p>
    SEO specialists moving toward content usually need to build editorial judgment and voice through real
    practice, since auditing a page about a topic is a different skill from deciding what that page should
    actually say and how it should sound.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      Neither switch is instant, and neither switch is rare. Treat your first choice as a strong starting
      lane, not a life sentence — the overlap lane between these two careers is wider than most comparison
      charts suggest.
    </p>
  </div>

